WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:03.913
Okay, today we are talking space planes.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:10.900
A very weird, sci-fi sort of idea.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.210
They are not quite planes,
they're not quite rockets,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.680
but hopefully they will
be a cheap and quick way

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.150
to get things to space, and
the company we're gonna visit

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.580
that's making one of these space planes

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.060
is called Dawn Aerospace.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.400
They're based in New Zealand.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.973
One of the co-founders is
a guy named Stefan Powell.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.150
The company is pretty young,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.720
they've just built their first prototype.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.260
And so with that, let's head
way to the bottom of the world

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.363
and Christchurch and talk to Stefan.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.740
We're at this interesting
time, and you're in

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.210
an interesting place when
it comes to rocketry,

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:57.940
there's a little bit of a race going on

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.440
to make these small rockets that are cheap

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.097
and that you could launch all the time.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:08.520
Rocket Lab, which has got its
main operations in Auckland,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.040
is the clear leader so far.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.440
They're the only ones
that have really launched.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.930
Then you guys have almost
like an even more radical take

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.480
on getting rapid access to space.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.030
So I mean, give me the 10,000 foot view

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:23.970
of what Dawn's trying to do.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:27.238
Yeah, so Dawn is a space
transportation company

00:01:27.238 --> 00:01:29.550
in a very general sense.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.360
You know, that's space launch
that's maneuvering things

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.700
around in space, and eventually
that means getting stuff

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.650
back down to Earth as well.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.600
And, okay, and then in terms of like,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.550
getting stuff to space,
I mean, you guys have

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.810
this awesome space plane, and
give me like, sorta the specs,

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.960
on like the first one
that you're building.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.430
Yeah, so the first plane
that we're building,

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:53.230
the Mark-II Aurora, is about
280 kilograms takeoff weight,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.410
about 2.5 meter wingspan, and it flies

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.813
out of regular airports, that's the point.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.050
Our space plane will be
certified under aircraft law,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.610
we'll fly it as an aircraft,
and so we will have a license

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.500
to fly the vehicle as opposed
to a rocket launch license

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.613
for that one flight.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.250
Yeah, well, and so for
people that, who like,

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.240
know nothing about this,

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.070
I mean before every rocket launch,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.970
it's like, you've gotta
apply for this license,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.450
which is this whole
timely and costly process,

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.580
and then you've got to clear the airspace.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.000
You've got to clear the water.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.840
And I mean, it is kind of
reasonable because these rockets

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.263
the way they are now,
they are pretty dangerous.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:36.943
Like a lot of them do fail.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.033
But aircraft are different,
just 'cause they have wings.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.575
You can completely lose
all your propulsion

00:02:44.575 --> 00:02:47.120
and you can generally still glide back.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:48.950
You know, it's not end of the mission.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.190
So with this first space
plane you guys are building,

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.320
how much will you take to space?

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.030
And then, and then what's kind of the goal

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.520
as you make bigger and
bigger space places?

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.790
Yeah, so, the Mark-II
Aurora will be able to take

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.993
four kilograms on a suborbital flight.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.130
The Mark-III should be more
like a business jet size,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.960
that'll be able to get about
50 to 100 kilograms into orbit.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.952
It ascends just like a
really high powered aircraft,

00:03:14.952 --> 00:03:16.960
by the time it gets to
the edge of the atmosphere

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.800
it's flying pretty much like a rocket.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.680
Once it gets to about
100-ish kilometers altitude

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.770
it'll release a second stage,
just like a regular rocket.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.880
That second stage is
gonna fly off into orbit

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.500
and deliver a satellite.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.610
And it can actually turn
around and glide back

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.980
to the launch site and
land just like an aircraft.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.440
In sci-fi, we read about the idea

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:36.965
of space plane all the time, but yeah,

00:03:36.965 --> 00:03:40.490
why hasn't somebody
built one of these so far

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:43.030
and why are you guys,
why do you guys think

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.640
you could actually do this?

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.303
They actually did try to.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.780
The Air Force, in particular,
started on space planes.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.340
The X-15 roars free on a tongue of flame!

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.080
That power is enough to shoot
nearly 59 miles into space!

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.640
But it eventually all fell
over because we just had to go

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.180
beat the Russians to space, you know,

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.223
and the easiest way to
do that was rockets.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.260
We revisited that with the space shuttle,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.940
yeah, that's a huge space
plane, that thing is massive.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.150
But it actually had lost every element

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:19.420
that would have made it an aircraft.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:21.300
In every other way it was a rocket,

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.300
it's just that it had wings for landing.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.240
There were then a whole
bunch of other programs,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.670
but in the end they all fell
over because the government

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.470
no longer wanted to back them.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.023
But the reason you can do
a space plane project now

00:04:34.023 --> 00:04:36.720
it because of the small satellite market.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.620
You know, we don't have
to build something capable

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:40.440
of taking 20 tons to orbit,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.890
hell, 20 kilograms would be fine.

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:45.410
And then the, just the
amount of venture capital

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.602
going into space now has gone up hugely,

00:04:48.602 --> 00:04:52.170
and so there is actually
commercial appetite for this stuff.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.640
And it's this kind of confluence of things

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.377
that makes space planes
actually make sense now.

00:04:57.377 --> 00:04:59.770
I think the big inflection point for us

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:01.750
is being able to show that yeah, we've had

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.453
properly reusable space launch technology.

00:05:04.453 --> 00:05:07.690
We can go fly a plane that is a plane

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:10.690
in every single way, but it has
the performance of a rocket.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.900
And if we can do that
basically on fuel cost,

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.080
plus a little bit of engineering time,

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.520
that's the game changer.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.873
Because you're going to cut
your total flight costs by 96%.

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:27.170
The price you guys are
talking about for a launch,

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.030
I mean, say you've taken
it from millions of dollars

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.823
to thousands of dollars to
get something into space.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.200
It sort of like opens up this new world

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.580
to run all these experiments
that people have been

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.337
clamoring to do.

00:05:40.337 --> 00:05:44.170
We found that molecules do weird things

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:45.530
when there's no gravity,

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.640
we found that the plants
grow in strange ways,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:53.000
DNA behaves differently and materials

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.830
can be made to do different things.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.470
Absolutely, and that's
why we're working on

00:05:58.470 --> 00:05:59.983
such a far out technology.

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:03.750
In-space manufacturing is
gonna be the next big thing.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.310
You know, multi-trillion dollar industries

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.040
could spawn out of this.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.680
We need space planes, we need
proper frictionless access

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.230
to space to actually make this happen.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.190
We want to be able to make
space more accessible.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:17.960
I mean, that's kind of our whole ethos,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.960
you know, space is kind of for everyone.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:22.300
And I saw, I think I saw

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.660
that you guys do this kind
of cool thing where you,

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.520
you put out is almost like a contest

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.093
that people could do the design

00:06:29.093 --> 00:06:31.868
on the outside of the, of the space plane.

00:06:31.868 --> 00:06:33.880
Was that just for students?

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.380
How did, how did that work?

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:36.932
No, it was, it was open to everyone.

00:06:36.932 --> 00:06:39.500
We had students like, you
know, we had 10 year old kids

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.482
put in designs as well as design studios

00:06:42.482 --> 00:06:45.730
and all kinds of people
from all over the world.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.350
Space is something the whole world

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.170
should be participating in.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:52.700
And so why not open that
up to everyone else?

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.676
And you had contest on
the payload too, right?

00:06:54.676 --> 00:06:57.530
Yeah, yeah, so the prize was actually

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.105
that if you designed
the winning paint job,

00:07:00.105 --> 00:07:01.990
we would fly payload for you.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.840
But funnily enough,
the company that won it

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.660
didn't have too much
interest in flying a payload.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:06.900
So we actually found another

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.592
contestant that had the best payload,

00:07:08.592 --> 00:07:10.932
Centaurus School in Colorado,

00:07:10.932 --> 00:07:14.843
and they want to measure
pollutants in the high atmosphere.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:18.960
Were you like a space geek, growing up

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:20.180
and you knew you always wanted to do this,

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:21.808
like, what were you into as a kid?

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:24.193
No, like I wasn't really a space geek.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:27.650
I actually went to the university thinking

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:30.250
that I was going to get
into sustainable technology,

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.510
wind turbines and whatever.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.760
But I actually got to the
university and realized that

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:37.750
there's not much practical
stuff you can do with them.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.390
It's not particularly hands-on

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.590
but there was this really
cool university group

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:43.783
building rockets.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.070
I could get stuck in there and
actually start building stuff

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.260
and putting solid rocket motors together.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.847
That was an epic experience, yeah.

00:07:54.922 --> 00:07:57.550
And one thing I wanted to talk about, too,

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:01.180
is you guys, you want to
make basically simpler

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.580
cheaper thrusters for satellites.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.549
Yeah, so scalable, high-performance,

00:08:06.549 --> 00:08:09.330
nontoxic chemical propulsion for cube sets

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.100
and small satellites.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.280
We can mass produce these,
they're relatively simple.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.240
They're relatively easy to put together.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:16.590
They click onto this satellite

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.508
and they can give mobility
to their constellation.

00:08:19.508 --> 00:08:21.107
That's a mature product, you know?

00:08:21.107 --> 00:08:23.524
You can go online and buy it.

00:08:23.524 --> 00:08:25.367
We actually have it in our webshop.

00:08:25.367 --> 00:08:28.070
You actually add to cart
and buy a rocket motor

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:29.930
with a credit card, if you want.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:31.330
So you guys have a couple
of things going on,

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.913
you're doing the business
around the thrusters.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:38.999
And then the biggest
milestone is the space plane

00:08:38.999 --> 00:08:41.550
to start running some
of these flight tests

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:42.630
as soon as you can.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:43.463
Yeah, exactly.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:48.310
If we're successful that will
then be the first vehicle

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.803
to ever be able to fly
to space twice in a day.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.480
We think that's kind of
the game-changing moment.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.100
That's the, like, okay, you know,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.936
we've actually got properly
reusable space systems.

